---
Part X, Chapter 12: The documentation shell
Package: site/src/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The documentation shell

The documentation reads nothing it was given by hand. Its own corpus of prose sits beside this one, `loom weave` renders that corpus to JSON before the site builds, and this chapter draws the result.

A chapter of the docs is drawn the way the site's other pages are drawn. It opens on a head: the Loom mark beside the chapter's name, its opening paragraph, and any commands it starts with set beside that paragraph. Its first section opens on a woven flower beside its heading, as the landing's sections do. The sections after it open on their heading alone. The chapter ends on a pager that names the chapters either side of it. Beside the sections a menu card lists every chapter in the docs, with the sections of the one on show under its name.

The shell is not tied to one corpus. Every function here takes the corpus it should read, and the page that calls it decides which one that is, so a second page over a second corpus could reuse it without a line changing.

## The woven corpus, read

A weave is data, so the shell begins by saying what shape that data has. A page is a list of blocks, and a block is either a heading, carrying its depth, its title and the slug to link at, or a run of prose carrying Markdown.

The types are declared here rather than imported because the weave is a build artifact. The JSON arrives typed as whatever it literally contains, which is too narrow to hold a second chapter and too wide to be worth reading.

=>

import { Array, Match, Option, pipe } from 'effect'
import { marked } from 'marked'
import type { Html, HtmlBuilder } from 'foldkit/html'
import {
  ClosedDrawer,
  Copied,
  OpenedChapter,
  PickedRuntime,
  SelectedSection,
  type Message,
  type Model,
  type Runtime,
} from './model'
import {
  broadSeam,
  bunIcon,
  type Carpet,
  carpetMark,
  checkIcon,
  copyIcon,
  flowerFor,
  loomMark,
  nodeIcon,
} from './components'
import { commandButton, installLine } from './hero'
import { loomCanvas } from './example'
import wovenDocs from './docs.woven.json'

type HeadingBlock = {
  readonly type: 'heading'
  readonly level: number
  readonly title: string
  readonly id: string
}

type ProseBlock = {
  readonly type: 'prose'
  readonly markdown: string
}

type Block = HeadingBlock | ProseBlock

type Chapter = {
  readonly number: string
  readonly title: string
  readonly slug: string
}

type Part = {
  readonly number: string
  readonly name: string
  readonly chapters: ReadonlyArray<Chapter>
}

type Page = {
  readonly slug: string
  readonly title: string
  readonly blocks: ReadonlyArray<Block>
}

export type Corpus = {
  readonly nav: ReadonlyArray<Part>
  readonly pages: ReadonlyArray<Page>
}

export const docsCorpus = wovenDocs as unknown as Corpus

~

A few lookups serve the whole chapter. One finds the page a slug names, one finds the part that page belongs to, and one lists every chapter in reading order, parts and all. Each answers with an `Option` where a slug might name nothing, so a slug naming nothing stays a value rather than a crash.

Where no chapter is named the shell opens the first one in the corpus, which is what a reader arriving at the documentation root expects.

=>

export const firstSlugOf = (corpus: Corpus): string =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.pages[0]),
    Option.map((page) => page.slug),
    Option.getOrElse(() => ''),
  )

const pageOf = (corpus: Corpus, slug: string): Option.Option<Page> =>
  Array.findFirst(corpus.pages, (page) => page.slug === slug)

const partOf = (corpus: Corpus, slug: string): Option.Option<Part> =>
  Array.findFirst(corpus.nav, (part) =>
    Array.some(part.chapters, (chapter) => chapter.slug === slug),
  )

const readingOrder = (corpus: Corpus): ReadonlyArray<Chapter> =>
  Array.flatMap(corpus.nav, (part) => part.chapters)

## A chapter, cut into its parts

The weave hands over a chapter as one flat run of blocks. The shell needs it in three parts: the chapter's title, its opening, and its sections. The title is the one first-level heading. The opening is the prose between the title and the first second-level heading. A section is a second-level heading and every block after it, up to the next second-level heading.

A third-level heading does not start a section. It stays inside the section it falls in and draws as a smaller heading there.

=>

type Section = {
  readonly heading: HeadingBlock
  readonly blocks: ReadonlyArray<Block>
}

type Cut = {
  readonly title: Option.Option<HeadingBlock>
  readonly opening: ReadonlyArray<Block>
  readonly sections: ReadonlyArray<Section>
}

const isTitle = (block: Block): block is HeadingBlock =>
  block.type === 'heading' && block.level === 1

const isSectionHead = (block: Block): block is HeadingBlock =>
  block.type === 'heading' && block.level === 2

const grow = (sections: ReadonlyArray<Section>, block: Block): ReadonlyArray<Section> =>
  isSectionHead(block)
    ? Array.append(sections, { heading: block, blocks: [] })
    : pipe(
        Array.last(sections),
        Option.match({
          onNone: () => sections,
          onSome: (last) =>
            Array.append(Array.dropRight(sections, 1), {
              ...last,
              blocks: Array.append(last.blocks, block),
            }),
        }),
      )

const cutOf = (page: Page): Cut => {
  const body = Array.filter(page.blocks, (block) => !isTitle(block))
  const [opening, rest] = Array.span(body, (block) => !isSectionHead(block))
  return {
    title: Array.findFirst(page.blocks, isTitle),
    opening,
    sections: Array.reduce(rest, [] as ReadonlyArray<Section>, grow),
  }
}

## Prose and the fences inside it

A run of prose is Markdown, and Markdown becomes HTML here rather than at weave time. The weave should stay a description of the document, not a description of a web page, so anything else reading that JSON gets the Markdown intact.

Not every fence in that Markdown is drawn as Markdown would draw it. A fenced command becomes a button that copies it, and a quoted loom becomes the landing's highlighted card. So `piecesOf` first cuts a run of prose into pieces: stretches of text, and the fences between them, each fence carrying the word after its opening backticks and the lines it holds. A stretch of text that is only blank space is dropped, so two fences in a row leave nothing between them.

=>

type Piece =
  | { readonly type: 'text'; readonly markdown: string }
  | { readonly type: 'fence'; readonly kind: string; readonly body: string }

const FENCE = /^```([\w-]*)[ \t]*\r?\n([\s\S]*?)^```[ \t]*$/m

const piecesOf = (markdown: string): ReadonlyArray<Piece> => {
  const [first, ...rest] = markdown.split(FENCE)
  return Array.filter(
    [
      { type: 'text', markdown: first ?? '' } as Piece,
      ...Array.flatMap(Array.chunksOf(rest, 3), ([kind, body, after]): ReadonlyArray<Piece> => [
        { type: 'fence', kind: kind ?? '', body: body ?? '' },
        { type: 'text', markdown: after ?? '' },
      ]),
    ],
    (piece) => piece.type === 'fence' || piece.markdown.trim() !== '',
  )
}

const textOf = (markdown: string, className: string, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div([h.Class(className), h.InnerHTML(marked.parse(markdown) as string)], [])

## Drawing a fence

The word after a fence's backticks decides how the fence draws.

- An empty fence tagged `install` draws the landing's install line in its place: the same tabs, the same commands, the same copy button.
- A fence tagged `run` names a file, and draws a picker that runs it: a Bun tab and a Node tab over one command that copies.
- A `bash` fence that holds a single command becomes the landing's copy button on its own, so a reader copies a command rather than selecting it.
- A `loom` fence becomes a card in the example's colours, so a loom quoted in the docs reads the way the landing shows one.
- Every other fence, a `bash` script of several lines included, becomes a code sheet: the same card as the loom, holding the code as plain text, with the same copy button in its corner.

=>

const fenceOf = (
  kind: string,
  body: string,
  model: Model,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html =>
  Match.value(kind).pipe(
    Match.when('install', () => installLine(model, h)),
    Match.when('run', () => runLine(body.trim(), model, h)),
    Match.when('loom', () => loomCanvas(body, model.copied, h)),
    Match.when('bash', () => commandsOf(body, model, h)),
    Match.orElse(() => codeSheet(body, model.copied, h)),
  )

const commandsOf = (body: string, model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const lines = pipe(
    body.split('\n'),
    Array.map((line) => line.trim()),
    Array.filter((line) => line !== ''),
  )
  return pipe(
    Array.head(lines),
    Option.filter(() => lines.length === 1),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => codeSheet(body, model.copied, h),
      onSome: (command) =>
        h.div([h.Class('docs-command')], [commandButton(`command:${command}`, command, model.copied, h)]),
    }),
  )
}

~

The code sheet copies exactly the code it shows, without the newline the fence leaves at its end. Its button shows a tick once pressed and turns back to the copy glyph a moment later, as the loom card's does.

=>

const codeSheet = (body: string, copied: string, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const code = body.replace(/\n$/, '')
  const id = `code:${code}`
  const taken = copied === id
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('docs-canvas')],
    [
      h.button(
        [
          h.Class(taken ? 'canvas-copy done' : 'canvas-copy'),
          h.Type('button'),
          h.Title(taken ? 'Copied' : 'Copy the code'),
          h.AriaLabel('Copy the code'),
          h.OnClick(Copied({ id, text: code })),
        ],
        [taken ? checkIcon() : copyIcon()],
      ),
      h.pre([h.Class('sheet-code')], [h.code([], [code])]),
    ],
  )
}

~

The run picker follows the install line's choice rather than keeping one of its own. A reader who picked Bun to install Loom runs the file with Bun, and a reader who picked npm or pnpm runs it with Node. Pressing Node picks npm, and pressing Bun picks Bun, so the two pickers on a page always agree.

=>

type Runner = {
  readonly runtime: Runtime
  readonly label: string
  readonly logo: Html
}

const bunRunner: Runner = { runtime: 'bun', label: 'bun', logo: bunIcon() }
const nodeRunner: Runner = { runtime: 'npm', label: 'node', logo: nodeIcon() }

const runnerOf = (runtime: Runtime): Runner =>
  Match.value(runtime).pipe(
    Match.when('bun', () => bunRunner),
    Match.orElse(() => nodeRunner),
  )

const runLine = (file: string, model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const chosen = runnerOf(model.runtime)
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('hero-install')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('rt-tabs')],
        Array.map([bunRunner, nodeRunner], (runner) =>
          h.button(
            [
              h.Class(runner.label === chosen.label ? 'rt-tab here' : 'rt-tab'),
              h.Type('button'),
              h.OnClick(PickedRuntime({ runtime: runner.runtime })),
            ],
            [runner.logo, runner.label],
          ),
        ),
      ),
      commandButton(`run:${file}`, `${chosen.label} ${file}`, model.copied, h),
    ],
  )
}

## A section, under its heading

A block draws as one of two things and never a third, so the match closes. A heading inside a section draws as a third-level heading carrying its slug, and a run of prose draws as its pieces: each stretch of text as Markdown, each fence as `fenceOf` decides.

=>

const blockOf = (block: Block, model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): ReadonlyArray<Html> =>
  Match.value(block).pipe(
    Match.when({ type: 'heading' }, (heading) => [h.h3([h.Id(heading.id)], [heading.title])]),
    Match.when({ type: 'prose' }, (prose) =>
      Array.map(piecesOf(prose.markdown), (piece) =>
        piece.type === 'text'
          ? textOf(piece.markdown, 'docs-prose', h)
          : fenceOf(piece.kind, piece.body, model, h),
      ),
    ),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

~

The first section of a chapter opens on the chapter's flower beside its heading, as a section of the landing opens on its carpet. The sections after it open on their heading alone. One mark to a chapter is enough to say where a reader is, and a mark on every section would turn the docs into a row of stickers. Everything a section holds then runs in one column beneath its heading, in the order the chapter wrote it: a paragraph, then the command it introduces, then the next paragraph.

=>

const sectionOf = (
  flower: Option.Option<Carpet>,
  section: Section,
  model: Model,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('section doc-section')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('section-head')],
        [
          ...Option.toArray(Option.map(flower, (carpet) => carpetMark(carpet, '42px', broadSeam))),
          h.div([h.Class('lines')], [h.h2([h.Id(section.heading.id)], [section.heading.title])]),
        ],
      ),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('doc-stack')],
        Array.flatMap(section.blocks, (block) => blockOf(block, model, h)),
      ),
    ],
  )

## The head of a chapter

The head is the page head the site's other pages open on: the Loom mark at four fifths of its size beside the chapter's name. Above the name a line says where the chapter sits: the documentation, the part that holds it, then the chapter. The first two are buttons, one opening the first chapter of the docs and one the first chapter of the part.

=>

const crumbButton = (label: string, slug: string, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.button([h.Class('crumb'), h.Type('button'), h.OnClick(OpenedChapter({ slug }))], [label])

const breadcrumb = (corpus: Corpus, root: string, page: Page, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('eyebrow doc-crumbs')],
    [
      crumbButton(root, firstSlugOf(corpus), h),
      h.span([h.Class('sep'), h.AriaHidden(true)], ['/']),
      ...pipe(
        partOf(corpus, page.slug),
        Option.flatMap((part) =>
          Option.map(Array.head(part.chapters), (first) => [
            crumbButton(part.name, first.slug, h),
            h.span([h.Class('sep'), h.AriaHidden(true)], ['/']),
          ]),
        ),
        Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<Html> => []),
      ),
      h.span([h.Class('here')], [page.title]),
    ],
  )

~

The chapter's opening fills the rest of the head. Its text becomes the lead under the name. Its fences, when it has any, stand in a column of their own beside the lead, level with the line above the name. The quick start opens this way with the two commands a reader runs first, so they are on screen before the first section starts.

=>

const docHead = (
  corpus: Corpus,
  root: string,
  page: Page,
  cut: Cut,
  model: Model,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html => {
  const pieces = Array.flatMap(cut.opening, (block) =>
    block.type === 'prose' ? piecesOf(block.markdown) : [],
  )
  const heading = Option.getOrElse(cut.title, (): HeadingBlock => ({
    type: 'heading',
    level: 1,
    title: page.title,
    id: page.slug,
  }))
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('section section-hero page-hero doc-hero')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('doc-hero-grid')],
        [
          h.div(
            [h.Class('doc-hero-lead')],
            [
              breadcrumb(corpus, root, page, h),
              h.div(
                [h.Class('page-title')],
                [loomMark('paper', 0.8), h.h1([h.Id(heading.id)], [heading.title])],
              ),
              ...Array.flatMap(pieces, (piece) =>
                piece.type === 'text' ? [textOf(piece.markdown, 'docs-prose page-lead', h)] : [],
              ),
            ],
          ),
          h.div(
            [h.Class('hero-cli')],
            Array.flatMap(pieces, (piece) =>
              piece.type === 'fence' ? [fenceOf(piece.kind, piece.body, model, h)] : [],
            ),
          ),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )
}

## The menu of every chapter

The menu is the corpus's own table of contents, drawn on a pale card like the cards on the site's other pages. Each part is a label in small capitals, and each chapter under it is a button that opens it. The chapter on show sets in dark ink at a heavier weight, and its sections hang beneath it, smaller and indented.

Only the section a reader has reached takes the accent. It turns orange and wears a small square in front, so the menu marks one place, never two. A section counts as reached once its heading has scrolled up to the top of the window. Before any has, the first is marked.

The menu's groups come out of a function of their own, because the drawer draws exactly the same list on a narrow window. A reader who opens the drawer should find the menu they already know, current marker included, not a second navigation that merely resembles it.

=>

const sectionButton = (section: Section, here: boolean, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.button(
    [
      h.Class(here ? 'menu-sub here' : 'menu-sub'),
      h.Type('button'),
      h.OnClick(SelectedSection({ id: section.heading.id })),
    ],
    [section.heading.title],
  )

const reachedOf = (cut: Cut, active: string): string =>
  active !== ''
    ? active
    : pipe(
        Array.head(cut.sections),
        Option.map((section) => section.heading.id),
        Option.getOrElse(() => ''),
      )

const chapterRows = (
  chapter: Chapter,
  page: Page,
  cut: Cut,
  active: string,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): ReadonlyArray<Html> =>
  chapter.slug === page.slug
    ? [
        h.button([h.Class('menu-item here'), h.Type('button')], [chapter.title]),
        h.div(
          [h.Class('menu-subs')],
          Array.map(cut.sections, (section) =>
            sectionButton(section, section.heading.id === reachedOf(cut, active), h),
          ),
        ),
      ]
    : [
        h.button(
          [h.Class('menu-item'), h.Type('button'), h.OnClick(OpenedChapter({ slug: chapter.slug }))],
          [chapter.title],
        ),
      ]

const menuGroups = (
  corpus: Corpus,
  page: Page,
  cut: Cut,
  active: string,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): ReadonlyArray<Html> =>
  Array.map(corpus.nav, (part) =>
    h.div(
      [h.Class('menu-part')],
      [
        h.span([h.Class('eyebrow')], [part.name]),
        ...Array.flatMap(part.chapters, (chapter) => chapterRows(chapter, page, cut, active, h)),
      ],
    ),
  )

const menu = (corpus: Corpus, page: Page, cut: Cut, active: string, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.nav(
    [h.Class('menu-card thin-scroll'), h.AriaLabel('Chapters')],
    menuGroups(corpus, page, cut, active, h),
  )

## The pager between chapters

A chapter ends on the site's own pager, naming the chapters either side of it in reading order. A card is a flower and two lines of type, the direction and the chapter's name, and under the pointer both lines take the deep orange the source page answers to. The flower is the chapter's own, the one its first section wears, so a card shows a reader the mark they will meet on the page it leads to.

The first chapter has nothing before it and the last nothing after, so a pager can carry one card rather than two. A next card keeps to the right even on its own, as every next card on the site does.

=>

const chapterFlower = (corpus: Corpus, slug: string): Carpet =>
  flowerFor(
    pipe(
      Array.findFirstIndex(readingOrder(corpus), (chapter) => chapter.slug === slug),
      Option.getOrElse(() => 0),
    ),
  )

const chapterCard = (
  corpus: Corpus,
  chapter: Chapter,
  direction: 'Previous' | 'Next',
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html => {
  const mark = carpetMark(chapterFlower(corpus, chapter.slug), '42px', broadSeam)
  const words = h.div(
    [h.Class('pager-words')],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('eyebrow')], [direction]),
      h.span([h.Class('pager-title')], [chapter.title]),
    ],
  )
  return h.button(
    [
      h.Class(direction === 'Next' ? 'pager-card next' : 'pager-card'),
      h.Type('button'),
      h.Style({ '--pg-ink': '#C2410C' }),
      h.OnClick(OpenedChapter({ slug: chapter.slug })),
    ],
    direction === 'Next' ? [words, mark] : [mark, words],
  )
}

const chapterPager = (corpus: Corpus, page: Page, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const order = readingOrder(corpus)
  const at = Array.findFirstIndex(order, (chapter) => chapter.slug === page.slug)
  const step = (by: number): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
    Option.flatMap(at, (index) => Array.get(order, index + by))
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('pager-row doc-pager')],
    [
      ...Option.toArray(Option.map(step(-1), (chapter) => chapterCard(corpus, chapter, 'Previous', h))),
      ...Option.toArray(Option.map(step(1), (chapter) => chapterCard(corpus, chapter, 'Next', h))),
    ],
  )
}

## The drawer, where the menu will not fit

Below a thousand pixels there is no room for the menu beside the sections, so the same menu arrives in a drawer instead. It slides in from the left over a scrim, and it carries the Loom mark and wordmark at its head so a reader knows what they opened. The nav opens it: on the docs, a narrow window gives the nav a burger button at its left end.

Both the scrim and the panel stay in the document whether the drawer is open or shut, and open is a class rather than a condition. That is what lets them animate: an element that appears only when open has nothing to slide in from.

=>

const closeIcon = (h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.svg(
    [
      h.Width('15'),
      h.Height('15'),
      h.ViewBox('0 0 24 24'),
      h.Fill('none'),
      h.Stroke('currentColor'),
      h.StrokeWidth('2'),
      h.StrokeLinecap('round'),
      h.AriaHidden(true),
    ],
    [
      h.line([h.X1('5'), h.Y1('5'), h.X2('19'), h.Y2('19')], []),
      h.line([h.X1('19'), h.Y1('5'), h.X2('5'), h.Y2('19')], []),
    ],
  )

const drawer = (
  corpus: Corpus,
  page: Page,
  cut: Cut,
  model: Model,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class(model.drawerOpen ? 'docs-drawer open' : 'docs-drawer')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('drawer-head')],
        [
          loomMark('paper', 0.495),
          h.span([h.Class('drawer-word')], ['Loom']),
          h.button(
            [
              h.Class('drawer-close'),
              h.Type('button'),
              h.AriaLabel('Close menu'),
              h.OnClick(ClosedDrawer()),
            ],
            [closeIcon(h)],
          ),
        ],
      ),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('drawer-body thin-scroll')],
        menuGroups(corpus, page, cut, model.activeSection, h),
      ),
    ],
  )

const scrim = (open: boolean, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [
      h.Class(open ? 'docs-scrim open' : 'docs-scrim'),
      h.AriaHidden(true),
      h.OnClick(ClosedDrawer()),
    ],
    [],
  )

## The page, assembled

The shell stacks the head over a body of two columns, the menu on the left and the sections on the right, with the pager closing the right-hand column. The pager sits at the foot of that column, so once a reader has scrolled to the end, the pager's lower edge lines up with the menu's.

A slug naming no chapter falls back to the first, so the documentation always has something to show.

=>

export const wovenPage = (
  corpus: Corpus,
  root: string,
  model: Model,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html => {
  const slug = model.chapter === '' ? firstSlugOf(corpus) : model.chapter
  return pipe(
    pageOf(corpus, slug),
    Option.orElse(() => Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.pages[0])),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => h.div([h.Class('docs-page')], []),
      onSome: (page) => {
        const cut = cutOf(page)
        return h.div(
          [h.Class('docs-page')],
          [
            docHead(corpus, root, page, cut, model, h),
            h.div(
              [h.Class('docs-body')],
              [
                menu(corpus, page, cut, model.activeSection, h),
                h.article(
                  [h.Class('docs-article')],
                  [
                    ...Array.map(cut.sections, (section, place) =>
                      sectionOf(
                        place === 0 ? Option.some(chapterFlower(corpus, page.slug)) : Option.none(),
                        section,
                        model,
                        h,
                      ),
                    ),
                    chapterPager(corpus, page, h),
                  ],
                ),
              ],
            ),
            scrim(model.drawerOpen, h),
            drawer(corpus, page, cut, model, h),
          ],
        )
      },
    }),
  )
}

export const documentationPage = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  wovenPage(docsCorpus, 'Docs', model, h)

